Date: Thu, 4 Feb 93 18:02:58 EST
From: drw@BOURBAKI.MIT.EDU
Subject: ECON: Mr. Wilson's assertions

Andy Wilson <ajw@think.com> writes:

> Sam Walton was a shrewd businessman,  but he did a lot to destroy the
> culture of small-town America.  In Michigan,  where I'm originally from,
> many suburbs are banning strip-mall construction in order to fight the
> creeping homogeneity that is consuming the country.  Small-town life
> is disintegrating,  and with this collapse will come the problems
> with violence and degradation that have been associated with urban
> life.

(I grew up in a town of 9,000 people, Grinnell, Iowa, and I'm still
nostalgic about it.  But...)  Strip-mall-ism is disgusting, in my
mind, and I don't mind seeing zoning constrain it a bit.  (That's
not libertarian.)  However, Walton's genius was that he avoided cities
and suburbs, and realized that a discount store could serve a genuine
need in rural America as well.

As for homogeniety, small-town America has a strong element of
homogeniety.  That's one of the reason that it doesn't have so much
political strife -- the sharp differences in culture between ethnic
neighborhoods in cities, and the sharp differences in interests
between suburbs and cities, are just not present.  Also, in such a
small sphere, you may not know everybody, but any two people have a
mutual friend.  Disputes must be suppressed, or the entire community
will explode -- you will wind up meeting your political enemy while
you're buying socks.

Historically, Norman Rockwell small-town America existed only in rural
America between the coming of the railroad (1820 through 1880,
depending on location) and the massive movement to the cities in the
1960s.  That's why life was so pleasant there -- there was easy access
to manufactured goods, but the enormous growth of the very profitable
information-processing industries hadn't yet made city living
significantly better paid.

In recent years, the cities have offered much better jobs, mostly
because of the information-processing industries.  So people are
bleeding away from the small towns, looking for better opportunities
in exurbia.

> Bill Gates was in the right place at the right time,  and was able to
> keep control of the situation.  But no amount of money will buy him
> the basic interpersonal skills that he sorely lacks.

Well, I doubt that his lack of interpersonal skills bothers him too
much, or he would have spent more time worrying about it.  :-) But it
is clear that Gates had the ability to take advantage of the
opportunities in a way that many others didn't.  (Digital Research
pissed off IBM during the negotiations, so everybody now uses MS-DOS
rather than CP/M-86!)  Louis Pasteur, every five years or so, stumbled
onto something that revolutionized biology.  After a while, you begin
to suspect it was something about *him*, and not just luck.

> It's like saying that someone who wins at a game of poker somehow has
> some special quality that entitles them to the pot that the other players 
> didn't have.  It's a matter of social contract.  With a different set of 
> hands,  someone else probably would have won.

That's a truly strange statement.  The essence of winning at poker is
not the luck of drawing the right hand, but knowing when and how to
bet so that you make more money on the winning hands than you lose on
the losing hands.  If you think it's merely a matter of chance who
wins, a professional poker player (and there are many of them!) will
clean you out.  Business is very similar.

> What about Leona Helmsley,  Michael Milken or the scumbags who pulled 
> off the biggest heist in history in the savings and loan shell game?
> Did they all deserve their megamegamegabucks too?

Personally, I think Helmsley was a simple criminal, and got what she
deserved.  (But then, I don't object to taxes on principle.)

Milken is a more complicated example -- he combined great business
briliance with an unfortunate tendency to engage in crimes as well.
If he'd stuck to the former, he'd not be in jail, and he'd be the
toast of the economy.  For Milken created the junk bond, which
tranformed corporate America from a flabby old men's club into
something which can actually compete in the world market.  Before
Milken, the president of a company effectively appointed the board of
directors, who appoined him.  Thus, once you became president, you
were president-for-life.  This resulted in corporate managment of the
same quality as governments that have presidents-for-life: slothful
and corrupt.  In principle, the stockholders could get together and
vote in a new board, but there were thousands of stockholders, and
none of the owned enough stock to make it worth the bother of
organizing a revolt in any single case.  (Classic tragedy of the
commons.)

But Milken invented the junk bond.  Now it was possible for a raider
to *borrow* enough money to buy *all* the stock of a company, no
matter how large.  And if the present management was slothful and
corrupt, a raider could simply borrow the money to buy the company,
clean up the managment, and then sell it to pay off the bonds.  The
company was cleaned up, and the raider made a hefty profit.

After this happened to a number of companies, the presidents-for-life
of many other companies decided to enhance their longevity by no
longer being slothful and corrupt.

This has led to the massive "restructuring" of American industry,
which is now much more productive and competitive.  It has also led to
the laying off of millions of workers, which has caused a great deal
of pain, but that's a different issue.

The S&L mess is even more complicated, because there are several
factors:

- A large fraction of the losses were due to stupidity.  "Invest in
real estate; it can't go down!"  Enough said.

- Some S&L's were deliberately looted.  The people who did so were
criminals.  Fortunately, some of them are being tracked down and
jailed.  But thieves get away all the time.

- The core problem was that the federal insurance system for S&L's was
made into the worst possible combination of socialism and capitalism
-- the federal government bore the entire risk of loss, but had no
effective ability to control the S&L's, either through regulation or
through charging insurance pemiums that vary based on risk.  Some of
this was due to an over-enthusiastic embrace of partial deregulation
of the S&L's, and some of it was due to deliberate political
interference (notably by St. Germain and Jim Wright).

Dale

