Subject: The rights of the infected

   From: pmetzger@shearson.com (Perry E. Metzger)

   These questions, in general, have no one right answer. The way that
   they have been solved traditionally is by precedent. So long as the courts
   maintain a consistant standard and everyone understands what is expected
   of them, everything will be fine.

IMHO, the problems arise when some aspect of external circumstances
changes so as to upset the assumptions upon which the tradition is
based.  For instance, twenty years ago, dumping some CFCs into the
atmosphere just wasn't a problem; now we have to claim ownership of
the atmosphere as a whole.  At first, the EM spectrum was held in
common, and anybody could broadcast on any frequency they chose.  Then
it filled up, and suddenly a regulatory apparatus of some sort had to
be devised to allocate its use.  In this country, the federal
government seized control of it, but in any case, the earlier right to
free broadcasting on any frequency had to go...

