Date: Thu, 4 Feb 93 16:09:36 EST
From: pmetzger@shearson.com (Perry E. Metzger)
Subject: Drugs in a customary legal system

> From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
> 
> Perry Metzger:
> > Well, I could start by noting that things like illegal drugs won't be
> > illegal in a PPL environment...
> 
> This would require a vast attitude change, which wouldn't necessarily
> accompany a vast attitude change that brings us PPL.  Perhaps well over 
> half of the American public would sign up for PPLs that brag about
> being "drug-free".

PPL does not imply proprietary community, Nick. PPL systems themselves
cannot effectively ban drugs. Against whom is my taking a drug a tort?
It certainly isn't a tort in any reasonable sense of the word. Its
certainly possible some people would choose to live in buildings or
proprietary communities where drug taking was illegal, but people CHOOSE
to live in such a community, and intentionally move into it. Presumably
if they want to take drugs they could, in any case, drive down the street
and light up their crack pipe safely outside of the condo or whatever
it is that they are living in that prohibits drug taking.

> These PPLs would put a high priority on shielding 
> their clients, their client's children, etc. from access to drug markets.  

Again, I think that you are confusing PPL with proprietary community
and confusing contracts with legal systems. Certainly a security company
could not and would not spend its time stopping people from taking drugs --
thats not a tort. Presumably, someone could sign a contract that would
prevent them from taking drugs, and then they would owe someone damages
if they did -- but thats a very different sort of situation.

> For example, any real estate owner could ban substances within its 
> boundary, and hire an agency to enforce this convenant among the tenants.  
> If the property is large and its tenants wealthy, this opens up a niche 
> for drug smuggglers, who would be considered by most people "criminals".

The term criminal is deceptive here. There are no crimes under a PPL system,
only torts. One can be a tort feasor, but not a criminal. Certainly were
someone to open up a very large proprietary community, people might try
to break the community rules in certain regards. However, its hardly
"drug smuggling" when all you have to do is drive five miles to the local
"Pot & Crack Unlimited" and buy a pack of joints and a few vials of crack.
Its far more analagous to the case of a university where drinking is prohibited
on campus and people bring flasks of Jack Daniels from the liquor store
outside town. This is hardly "smuggling" in the traditional sense. Among
other things, you can't be thrown into jail for 50 years for it -- at
worst, you would end up paying some sort of damages, and I'd bet under
any reasonable system they'd end up being trivial.

Beyond all this, though, I dispute your notion that people in a PPL would
try to enforce such rules as a whole. Yes, there are places like IBM where
if you bring a bottle of booze into the parking lot you can get fired -- but
there are far more companies and condo complexes in the country (the vast
majority) where no one gives a damn. I doubt that many communities will
be built that have such strictures -- they'd lose too much money, and
besides, few people throw restrictive strictures on right now except to
stop people from disturbing the peace. Similarly, I'd doubt your contention
that a PPL system could be built with as few changes in societal attitude
as you claim. I usually assume that radical changes would have to take
place in people's willingness to keep their noses out of other people's
business in such a society -- and for good reason, as without such
an attitude the entire notion of government is not going to go away.

> > I doubt that outlaws
> > would survive well in a PPL environment, but certainly your point is
> > correct -- no system will work if there is strong dissent against it
> > by a very large minority. 
> 
> The world is like that -- divided between Jew and Arab, Catholic
> and Protestant, etc.  Unbundling territory from such conflicts
> may improve the situation; it's worth looking at.

Catholics and Protestants for the most part stopped their religious
warfare after the treaty of westphalia. Its true that there is still
some such conflict in Northern Ireland, but its largely residual and
the result, I'd argue, of British mismanagment, as is, by the way, the
current middle east conflict and the conflicts taking place in India.
In general, religious warfare and the like can't last for very long. In
history, we've generally seen that the practitioners get exhausted after
a generation or so of constant battling and eventually give up. There are
strong evolutionary pressures not to engage in pointless combat, not
the least of which is that dead men leave few kids. However, I digress.
My point was only that no system can bring peace if 10% of the population
is hell bent on suicide bombings. 

Perry

