Date: Thu, 4 Feb 93 15:45:43 PST
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: ECON/NANO: A short question

Ronald Hale-Evans asked:
>What, if any, objects, substances, or other entities, will still be in
>short supply and therefore valuable as commodities, in a world with
>"strong" nanotech (i.e. assemblers, etc.)?


jmeritt@mental.mitre.org answered: "Information"

Tom Morrow: "art and inventions ... "Authentic" artifacts. ... Pleasure ...
  [authentic non-AI] personal services"

Dani: "Status"

Blair Haworth: "raw materials [as] a function of "local"
  elemental abundance modified by demand ... information and energy" 

Richard Kennaway: "the ability to have lots of good ideas ... time
  ... a person with whom it will be a pleasure to associate."

Perry: "Energy and raw materials ... anything thats got a conservation law"

LUX owen: "*tasty* bit of information"


Given this list, perhaps we should ask what *won't* be valued, the we
do value now?  The only thing I don't see on this list is "capital",
raw material already arranged into structures, as opposed to just
having the instructions and a pile of raw material.  But I think this
too will be valued.

Perhaps more interesting to ask is how will the relative value of
different categories of things change?  We have talked about this
subject several times on this list before, and I am personally not in
the mood to go over the same ground at the same shallow level.  If,
however, anyone wants to get into some detail, I'm game.  (For
example, several times in the past I've tried to argue for dramatic
reductions in knowledge-worker wages relative to other factors of
production.)  Examples of what I'm not interested in are:

>I think that trade will break
>down a lot when we can personally manufacture all we need.  

> When the necessities and all the present-day luxuries of life
>are virtually free, the truly valuable must be puchased other than with
>money.

These are just silly, IMHO.  This image of an economy dominated by
physically-satiated humans wanting only art, good jokes, authentic
artificats, etc. is not even plausible.  

Robin Hanson


