"I'm sure that this can be argued both ways, but I think the
following bears scruitiny: to the extent that the decisions of the
Althing were practically binding on anyone, they were binding on  
everyone.  (There was no special class of people who were recognized
to have "opted out" of the Althing, and who had a greater practical
ability to defy the Althing than anyone else did.)" (Dale Worley)

In another message, someone, I am not sure whether Dale or someone
else, comments that Iceland had only one law code. Both propositions
are not quite true--the exception being a period of several hours in
the year 1000. At the peak of the Christian/Pagan conflict, the two
sides declared themselves "out of law with each other." Cooler heads
prevailed, both sides agreed on an arbitrator, and Iceland went back
to being a single legal system.

This does not, of course, contradict the central point, which is
correct--Iceland was a system of private law in the sense of a pure
civil system, in which litigation and the enforcement of verdicts
were private rather than state activities. It was not a system of
competing law codes. But I think that incident does say something
interesting about how the Icelanders themselves regarded their system.

Also, I should point out that the legislature was the Logretta, not
the Allthing. The Allthing was an annual assembly at which various
activities took place, including the meeting of the Logretta, some
but not all court procedings, lots of trading and socializing and
gathering of support for law cases and feuds, ... .

David Friedman

